steps.text:
You’ve found a real limitation/bug in that old Tailwind version’s `@apply` behavior.

`border-dotted` is represented by two generated rules:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; } /* reset */
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

With utility classes, `.border` is emitted between those rules, so it restores a non-zero width and the dotted border appears. But `@apply border-dotted` expands both `border-dotted` declarations into your component, leaving `border-width: 0`.

Apply a width after the style, preferably as a separate directive:

```css
.my-component {
  @apply border-dotted;
  @apply border;
}
```

or explicitly:

```css
.my-component {
  @apply border-dotted;
  border-width: 1px;
}
```

Putting both in one `@apply` is unreliable in that release, because it expands declarations according to generated CSS order, not necessarily the order you write the utility names.
